          "year": 2017,
          "date_decided": "2017-09-19",
          "holding": "The Court of Appeals affirmed a guardianship award, holding that the proposed guardian's sworn (if unspecific) testimony that she was willing and financially able to care for the child was competent evidence supporting the trial court's verification of adequate resources under N.C.G.S. § 7B-600(c).",
          "relevance": "N.H. governs the § 7B-600(c) verification that a guardian understands the appointment and has adequate resources.",
          "quote": "our standard of review merely asks if there was competent evidence, even hearsay evidence, at trial to support the trial court's findings",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/4427111/in-re-nh/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 4427111
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re E.R.",
          "citation": "248 N.C. App. 345, 795 S.E.2d 103 (2016)",
          "court": "Court of Appeals of North Carolina",
          "year": 2016,
          "date_decided": "2016-07-19",
          "holding": "The Court of Appeals reversed a permanency planning order awarding guardianship to non-relatives because the trial court made no findings explaining why placement with the children's paternal grandmother was not in their best interests, as N.C.G.S. § 7B-903(a1) requires, and held that ICWA compliance did not excuse those findings.",
          "relevance": "A guardianship award to non-relatives is reversible without findings rejecting an available relative placement.",
          "quote": "Such concerns, however, do not obviate the need for findings of fact under N.C. Gen. Stat. § 7B-903(a1) if the court chooses a nonrelative placement for a juvenile.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/7477123/in-re-er/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 7477123
        },
